   Singapore, SINGAPORE, FEB 26-28, 2010},
Organization = {IACSIT; Environm Sci Soc},
Abstract = {In the 21(st) century of modern development with the progress of
   civilization and rapid industrialization the problem of generation of
   large quantity of solid waste is become a concern in urban centers for
   Urban Local Bodies (ULB). Rapid industrial growth in urban developments
   may responsible for increase in living standards of residential
   population which is ultimately responsible for generation of huge
   quantity of solid waste. As per the census of 2001 the population of
   urban India was about 285 million which accounts the 27\% of the total
   population. On an average Urban centers in India generates about 1,
   20,000 tones of municipal solid waste per day.
   Aurangabad is one of the rapidly growing city in India and identified as
   capital of Marathwada having a population about 10 Lakh. The Aurangabad
   city has been divided into 6 zones and 98 electoral wards. The rapid
   industrial development gear up the urbanization which may ultimately
   results in production of large quantities of solid waste. The solid
   waste generation in Aurangabad city is approximately 350 Metric Tones
   (MT) per day.
   The present paper deals with the critical analysis of solid waste
   disposal in Aurangabad city. It covers existing status of the quantity
   of generated waste per day, collection, storage, transportation, and
   disposal system in Aurangabad city and the methods adopted by Aurangabad
   Municipal Corporation for the management of solid waste. It also focuses
   the lacunas in present solid waste disposal system and suggests the
   perspective ideas for proper and effective solid waste disposal system.},
