Title = {Knowledge Management Practices for Corporate Social Responsibility: A
   Family Business Perspective},
Booktitle = {PROCEEDINGS OF THE 15TH EUROPEAN CONFERENCE ON KNOWLEDGE MANAGEMENT
   (ECKM 2014), VOLS 1-3},
Series = {Proceedings of the European Conference on Knowledge Management},
Year = {2014},
Pages = {198-204},
Note = {15th European Conference on Knowledge Management (ECKM), Polytechn Inst
   Santarem, Santarem Sch Management \& Technol, Santarem, PORTUGAL, SEP
   04-05, 2014},
Abstract = {In the Knowledge Society, directly related to the creation, use and
   exchange of knowledge, many authors emphasize the relevance that social
   networks have within the business. Through the interaction and
   cooperation of stakeholders, companies have the opportunity to gain new
   information and knowledge, exploiting social capital. The development of
   social capital in the company favours the adoption of socially
   responsible practices and attitudes with business stakeholders.
   Stakeholders' perceptions about corporate social responsibility
   practices and social capital impact their subsequent behaviours and
   emotions and affect firm performance. The intangible assets related to
   the concepts of social capital and corporate social responsibility are
   at the basis of the firm performance. The study of the relationship
   between social capital, social responsibility and corporate performance
   has not been studied holistically in family businesses, although they
   are 85\% of all businesses in the Iberian Peninsula, according to the
   literature. Moreover, family firms conjugate features of cooperative
   stakeholder enterprises, where the role of noneconomic factors in the
   management of the firm is a key distinguishing feature that separates
   family firms from other organizational forms. Thus, considering the lack
   of consistent evidence, the present study is part of the drive that is
   needed to increase knowledge concerning the relationship between the
   corporate firm responsibility and social capital, and the relationship
   between social capital, corporate firm responsibility and firm
   performance in the context where there is some degree of family
   involvement in firm, as seen from the perspective of socioemotional
   wealth perspective. The preservation of the socioemotional wealth
   conduct to family control encourages families to favour the relationship
   between social capital and corporate firm responsibility. The results of
   our empirical study on 77 Spanish firms show that corporate firm
   responsibility have a positive influence on firm performance. Social
   Capital is positively related to corporate firm responsibility, and
   socioemotional wealth is positively related to social capital and
   corporate firm responsibility.},
