   (ECMLG), City Univ London, Cass Business Sch, London, ENGLAND, DEC
   11-12, 2017},
Abstract = {The rapid pace of change in all economic sectors and the nature of many
   occupations in the 21st century increasingly require the acquisition of
   various knowledge and skills through both curricular and extracurricular
   activities within the higher education institutions. Hard skills and
   soft skills are attributes that should be incorporated as early as
   possible in the students' experience because they are relevant for their
   future career. Policy makers, academic staff, students and employers are
   among the most interested stakeholders in obtaining both valuable
   employment outcomes and civic competences of higher education graduates.
   The goal of the paper is to show that the extracurricular activities can
   bring a significant contribution to the creation and development of
   leadership skills of students in the academic environment and to the
   growth of their employability level. The methodology is based on the use
   of theoretical and empirical research methods. The findings of the paper
   illustrates that extracurricular activities are beneficial activities
   for the development of leadership skills among students in the academic
   environment because they help students to become not only open,
   pro-active and flexible people, but also effective leaders.},
