   VANCOUVER, CANADA, SEP 21-25, 1998},
Organization = {Int Assoc Engn Geol \& Environm},
Abstract = {This paper outlines the potential of remote sensing and GIS techniques
   as tools for the management of areas exposed to industrial pollution
   phenomena. In particular the possible patterns of surface and ground
   waters pollution have been considered. Furthermore the interactions
   between industrial installations, water resources, protected areas and
   urban settlements have been analysed. Landsat TM images have been
   processed in order to obtain a land-use map that has been inserted in a
   GIS database and integrated with further information like lithology,
   geological structure, hydrogeological features, DEM, industrial plants
   location and characters. This allowed the creation and processing of
   different thematic maps: vulnerability, risk, impact.},
