Organization = {Lyndon State Coll; Mansfield Univ; Michigan State Univ; New Hampshire
   Div Parks \& Recreat; Penn State Univ; Soc Amer Foresters, Recreat
   Working Grp; SUNY Coll Environm Sci \& Forestry; Univ Massachusetts;
   Univ New Hampshire; Univ Vermont; Univ Waterloo; USDA Forest Serv, NE
   Forest Exptl Stn; Western Illinois Univ; Westfield State Coll; West
   Virginia Univ},
Abstract = {Attitudes have been debated as a contributing factor to behavior for
   decades. If this is the case, people's behavior in recreational settings
   should be partially influenced by their attitudes. Because many
   recreation activities occur in natural or other outdoor settings,
   environmental attitudes should be at least partially influential. A
   scale to measure a `'new environmental paradigm'' (NEP) has been
   developed to assess people's environmental attitudes. Using the NEP
   scale, this study assesses the influence of environmental attitudes as
   well as water pollution-related knowledge and two sociodemographic
   factors on Michigan recreational boaters' use of pumpout and dump
   facilities. Based on the Clean Vessel Act and state regulations, boaters
   are not allowed to dump sewage, treated or untreated, directly into
   inland or Great Lakes waters.},
