Note = {Conference on Urban Environmental Pollution - Overcoming Obstacles to
   Sustainability and Quality of Life, Boston, MA, JUN 20-23, 2010},
Abstract = {The effect of chemical nitrogen fertilizer application on groundwater
   arsenic pollution was examined. Arsenic and N concentrations in peat
   sediment were high. In the groundwater, a high arsenic concentration was
   observed under a high ammonium-N concentration with reducing conditions.
   From delta N-15 analysis, the source of N in peat sediment and
   groundwater was possibly chemical N fertilizer. Thus, we assumed that a
   high N environment created by the fertilizer led the groundwater to
   reducing conditions through the enhancement of microbial activity, and
   the reducing condition promoted the arsenic release from peat sediment
   to the groundwater. (C) 2009 Published by Elsevier BV},
