   the 21st Century, MALTA, 2008},
Organization = {Wessex Inst Technol; WIT Transact Built Environm},
Abstract = {Previously, Akamatsu et al. proposed ``tradable bottleneck permits{''}
   as a new transportation demand management scheme and proved the
   efficiency thereof for a general network. To implement such a scheme, we
   propose a multi-agent system for general transportation networks. The
   aim of this system is to achieve a socially optimal state in which the
   total transportation cost is minimized by the decentralized behaviour of
   agents. As a concrete step in designing the proposed system, we first
   define the micro behaviour of the agents. We also assume that the
   trading markets for bottleneck permits are described by a tatonnement
   process. We then derive day-to-day dynamics of aggregated traffic flows
   and permit prices. By analyzing the macro dynamics, we prove that the
   mean dynamics of the aggregated variables (flows and permit prices)
   converge to a socially optimal state.},
