   activity in the AM and the MD was recorded from rats trained to
   discriminate conditioned sensory stimuli associated with and without
   reinforcements, and from the naive rats. In the AM of trained rats there
   were topographic distributions of sensory-responsive neurons, which were
   well correlated to anatomical data. Furthermore, more sensory-responsive
   neurons were encountered in the AM of rats trained to associate sensory
   stimuli with a reinforcement, than in the AM of rats that were not
   trained. In trained rats, multimodal neurons that responded to
   conditioned and unconditioned stimuli were frequently found in the
   basolateral and central nuclei of the AM. The results suggest that
   basolateral and central nuclei are foci in which various sensory
   modalities converge, and that they might perform critical functions in
   the acquisition and storage of long-term associative memory, which links
   between sensory information and affective significance. In the rat MD,
   most neurons had multimodal responsiveness, and responded differentially
   to both elemental (auditory or visual stimulus alone) and configural
   (simultaneous presentation of auditory and visual stimuli) conditioned
   stimuli based on reward contingency. These multimodal differential MD
   neurons were located mainly in the rostromedial portion of the MD, which
   receives afferents from the basolateral nucleus of the AM, where Various
   sensory information converges. The present results, along with previous
   anatomical studies, suggest the existence of limbic emotional circuits:
   AM-medial MD-orbital prefrontal cortex (emotional memory).},
