Note = {2024 International Conference on Geographic Perspectives on Climate
   Change Mitigation in Urban and Rural Environments-GCUE, Lecce, ITALY,
   JUN 25-27, 2024},
Organization = {University of Salento},
Abstract = {The nexus and synergy between climate-smart agriculture (CSA) and soil
   organic carbon (SOC) has enhanced environmental sustainability in the
   food production sector. This work aimed to close the gap in knowledge
   between CSA and SOC and their potential in achieving a sustainable
   environment. Trends in the literature were unveiled on how the concepts
   were shaped and institutionalized by mapping their intellectual
   structure. The study provided answers to the where, when, and how of
   CSA-SOC nexus trends in scientific literature. From Web of science
   (WOS), the literature search was performed for the last 20 years by
   applying specific search, document selection criteria, and bibliometric
   coupling method. After excluding articles that were outside the
   goal/scope, a total of 478 papers were qualified for bibliometric
   analysis. Cluster analysis and bibliographic coupling algorithms were
   conducted using VOSviewer software. The result revealed that outside
   Brazil, authors and institutions from nineteen countries collaborated
   with the Brazilian authors. Year 2019 (having 49 articles) recorded the
   highest. In terms of impacts to the scientific community, Soil and
   Tillage Research (having Rv: 0.136) and Field Crops Research (Rv: 0.120)
   had the highest impacts. University of Sao Paulo and University of Rio
   Grande Do Sul had the highest number of citations and total link
   strength with 15.06\% and 10.25\% papers, respectively. Considering
   papers that focused on the Sustainable Development Goals (SDGs), SDGs
   02, 13, and 15 had the highest with 414 (38\%), 340 (31\%), and 324
   (30\%) papers, respectively. Findings from the study might support the
   Brazilian government in achieving its Nationally Determined
   Contributions (NDCs) as agreed at the Paris Summit where the country
   promised to tackle climate change by reducing carbon emissions and
   increasing C-sequestration through agricultural systems. This finding
   might contribute to narrow the gap in knowledge by enhancing targeted
   debate for effective discussion and policies for a better Brazil and
   society.},
