Abstract = {The eddy covariance method is a micrometeorological technique for direct
   measurements of gas transport between the surface and the atmosphere. It
   is widely used for quantifying CO2 emission rates from natural, urban
   and agricultural ecosystems, including areas of agricultural carbon
   sequestration. In geological carbon capture, utilization and
   sequestration, emerging projects utilize eddy covariance to monitor
   large areas where CO2 may escape from the subsurface, to detect and
   quantify CO2 leakage, and to improve CO2 storage efficiency. This paper
   describes key principles of the method, main requirements, typical
   instrumentation and software, and educational resources particularly
   useful for carbon sequestration research. (C) 2013 The Authors.
   Published by Elsevier Ltd.},
